      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "The push: potential difference"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "The battery gives each charge energy. The energy given up per unit charge across the resistor is the potential difference V, measured in volts with a voltmeter connected across the resistor."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "switchOn": true,
        "rheo": 0.25,
        "electrons": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Ohm's law"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Slide the rheostat. The current changes, and so does the voltmeter reading, but V divided by I stays the same. For a metal conductor at constant temperature, current is proportional to potential difference."
      },
      "equation": "V = IR",
      "modelState": {
        "switchOn": true,
        "rheo": 0.05,
        "electrons": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Inside a resistor"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Slice the board open. Inside the resistor is a long, thin resistive track. Electrons collide with its atoms as they pass, which is why a resistor limits the current and warms up."
      },
      "equation": "R = \\dfrac{V}{I}",
      "modelState": {
